<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=loom">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Loom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4400 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A loom is a device for weaving cloth and tapestry that holds the warp threads under tension while the weft threads are passed over and under them. The earliest evidence of a horizontal loom is a picture on a pottery dish from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> dated to 4400 BCE, showing a frame loom with treadles that left the weaver's hands free to pass and beat the weft. The warp-weighted loom, whose warp is kept taut by hanging weights, may go back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. The drawloom for patterned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> is first confirmed by fabrics from the State of Chu of about 400 BCE; it sped up Chinese silk weaving and was introduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/loom.jpg" alt="Loom" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G-Lignum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loom_2,_Lesbos_(Skala_Sykamias).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The word loom comes from the Old English geloma, which meant a utensil, tool or machine of any kind. Weaving is done with two sets of threads that cross one another. The warp threads are stretched on the loom, and each thread of the weft is inserted so that it passes over and under them. The ends of the warp are fastened to two beams, which are held apart to keep the threads taut.</p><p>A loom must perform three principal motions. Shedding pulls part of the warp aside to form a shed, the space through which the shuttle carries the weft. Picking inserts the weft, and battening beats the newest weft against the fell, the edge of the cloth. Lifting the odd and even threads alternately gives a tabby weave, while more complex sequences of sheds give weaves such as twill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Looms vary with the weaver's way of life: nomadic weavers tend to use light, portable looms, and those in cramped city dwellings a tall upright loom. The backstrap loom is a simple loom with ancient roots, still used in Andean textiles and in Central, East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. One bar is fixed to an object and the other to the weaver by a strap, so the weaver leans back to tension the warp.</p><p>The warp-weighted loom is a vertical loom that may have originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. Hanging weights keep bundles of warp threads taut, and the finished cloth can be rolled round the top beam. The earliest evidence of a horizontal loom is a pottery dish from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> dated to 4400 BCE. It shows a frame loom with treadles that lifted the warp threads, leaving the weaver's hands free to pass and beat the weft.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>John Kay patented the flying shuttle in 1733. A weaver flicked a picking stick joined by cords to the ends of the shed, and the shuttle shot across, so that one weaver could weave far wider cloth than an arm's length at much greater speed. Dandy looms, patented in 1802, rolled up the finished cloth automatically. Handlooms remained the most cost-effective way to make some textiles for most of the 1800s, even as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/power-loom">power looms</a> developed.</p><p>The loom is a symbol of cosmic creation and of the structure on which individual destiny is woven. In the classical myth of Arachne, the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> changed her into a spider out of jealousy of her skill at weaving. In Maya civilisation the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ix-chel">Ixchel</a> taught the first woman to weave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Loom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 4400 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The warp-weighted loom may have originated earlier, in the Neolithic period.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/punched-card">Punched card<span>1725</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/power-loom">Power loom<span>1785</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=loom">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
